def check_balanced_brackets(s):
    # Dictionary to store opening brackets
    open_brackets = {'(': '['}
    # Dictionary to store closing brackets
    close_brackets = {')': '(', '}': '{'}
    # Stack to keep track of opening brackets
    stack = []

    # Iterate through each character in the string
    for char in s:
        # If the character is an opening bracket, push it onto the stack
        if char in open_brackets:
            stack.append(char)
        # If the character is a closing bracket
        elif char in close_brackets:
            # If the stack is empty or the top of the stack doesn't match the corresponding opening bracket, return False
            if not stack or stack.pop() != close_brackets[char]:
                return False

    # If the stack is empty, all opening brackets had matching closing brackets
    return len(stack) == 0
```

This function uses a stack to keep track of the opening brackets. For each character in the string, it checks if it is an opening bracket or a closing bracket. If it is an opening bracket, it is pushed onto the stack

